    "result": "I implemented `shipping_cost` in `solution.py` and wrote `tests/test_solution.py`. `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all 79 tests. I could not run coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit checks because the only shell command allowed was the test command, so those gates are unverified. By reading the code, every line should be executed by the tests, and each function's cyclomatic complexity is at most 6, so CRAP should equal complexity, well under 10. The implementation follows the spec: inputs are converted to `Decimal` (floats via `str()`, `bool` rejected), billable weight is the larger of actual and volumetric weight rounded up to 0.5 kg, the base charge is 8.00 plus 1.50 per further half-kg, then the zone factor, free shipping for zones 1–2 at a subtotal of 150 or more, the 12.00 oversize surcharge for a longest side over 120 cm, and a final `ROUND_HALF_UP` to two places. The arithmetic runs in a 60-digit local context so the volumetric division cannot be rounded across a half-kg boundary. The tests check exact expected totals at each boundary (120 cm, 150 subtotal, 0.5 kg steps, a volumetric value just above 2.5 kg that catches a wrong divisor, and a 15.625 case that catches banker's rounding), plus the type and value error cases. I wrote the test expectations by hand from the spec rather than from the implementation, and one of my own expectations was wrong and was corrected before the run.",
